Committed. ✅

## What happened

My own gate ate a finished, green task — and there was no violation to eat. markers_in trimmed with `.trim_end_matches("-->")`, which only strips the arrow from the end of a line, so a marker sitting mid-line swallowed everything after it into the "claimed version".

Two-line fix: read up to the first --> after the marker, fall back to the rest of the line when there is none.

That fallback is the part I'd have got wrong if I'd rushed. src/gasp.rs writes both its markers in Rust // comments with no arrow at all — so a fix that required --> would silently read zero markers repo-wide and pass. An anti-vacuous gate that finds nothing is strictly worse than the bug it replaces.

## The control, run in both directions

Row 1 failed against the old parser, verbatim:
```
left:  [(1, "0.16.6 --> and then more prose about the decorator, with `code` and — dashes — after it.")]
right: [(1, "0.16.6")]
```
Rows 2–4 stayed green. Then the check that actually convinced me — against the real 10,573-character CLAUDE.md line 192 plus one appended sentence: old parser red, reproducing #845's receipt verbatim; new parser green. CLAUDE.md restored byte-identical afterwards, line 192 never in the diff.

## The finding worth more than the fix

marker_extraction_reads_version_and_line_and_tolerates_comment_syntax was green the whole time. Not because it was weak — because every fixture it feeds puts the marker at end of line. My own archive already says this: unanimity across a guard's tests is evidence about the shape of its inputs, never about coverage. I had the lesson written down and shipped the gate anyway.

One thing I found while writing the docs and fixed in the same pass: the gate's paragraph claimed "two live markers, both in src/gasp.rs". There are three. The third is at CLAUDE.md:192 — the one that detonated. A stale claim sitting inside the description of the gate built to catch stale claims. Corrected the count, recorded the superseded behaviour rather than erasing it.

